  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "case: Pattern Dispatch & the read Loop",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Ordering Kills"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Put *) Last",
          "text": "`*)` is the catch-all default. Placed in the middle it swallows everything, and every branch after it is dead code."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Branch Terminators"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "End",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`;;`",
              "stop, leave the case"
            ],
            [
              "`;&`",
              "run next body blindly"
            ],
            [
              "`;;&`",
              "keep testing patterns"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`;&` and `;;&` need Bash 4+",
            "`;;&` lets several branches match one value",
            "`shopt -s nocasematch` also affects `[[ ]]`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where case Shines"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "case $1 in",
            "  -h|--help) usage ;;",
            "  -v) verbose=1 ;;",
            "  -*) die \"unknown $1\" ;;",
            "esac"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Why Not if/elif",
          "items": [
            "Flatter than a chain testing one variable",
            "Faster to scan, harder to get wrong",
            "Status = last command of the matched branch",
            "Status `0` when nothing matched at all"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The One Correct Idiom",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "while IFS= read -r line; do",
            "  printf '%s\\n' \"$line\"",
            "done < file"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`IFS=` stops `read` stripping leading/trailing whitespace",
            "`-r` stops backslash interpretation",
            "Without it `C:\\path` loses slashes, `\\` at EOL joins lines",
            "The `IFS=` prefix applies to `read` only — no cleanup"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why the Loop Ends"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`read` returns non-zero at EOF — that terminates it",
            "A file with no trailing newline still sets `line`",
            "But `read` returns 1, so that last line is dropped"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "while IFS= read -r line || [[ -n $line ]]; do",
            "  echo \"$line\"",
            "done < file  # keeps last unterminated line"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Fields and Descriptors"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "while IFS=: read -r user pass uid rest; do",
            "  echo \"$user has uid $uid\"",
            "done < /etc/passwd   # rest = all leftovers"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "find . -print0 | while IFS= read -rd '' f; do",
            "  mv -- \"$f\" \"${f// /_}\"",
            "done  # survives newlines in filenames"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "read Flags, Subshells & Operator Spacing",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Extra fields all land in the LAST variable",
            "A nested read needs its own descriptor: `exec 3< other.txt`",
            "Then use `read -u 3` inside the outer loop"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Subshell Trap"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "cmd | while",
                "lines": [
                  "loop runs in a subshell",
                  "variable writes vanish",
                  "counter ends up 0"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "done < <(cmd)",
                "lines": [
                  "loop stays in this shell",
                  "variables survive",
                  "or `shopt -s lastpipe`"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "read Flags Worth Knowing"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flag",
            "Does"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`-t N`",
              "timeout, seconds"
            ],
            [
              "`-n N`",
              "read N chars"
            ],
            [
              "`-d ''`",
              "read until NUL"
            ],
            [
              "`-u 3`",
              "read from fd 3"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "When Not to Loop",
          "items": [
            "`mapfile -t lines < file` slurps it all at once",
            "`readarray` is the same builtin, other name",
            "`while read` is slow: roughly a syscall per line",
            "Pure text transforms belong in `awk` / `sed`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Redirect the Loop",
          "text": "`done < file` opens the file once and is the only place the input belongs — never redirect each command inside the body."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Body Steals the Input"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`ssh`, `ffmpeg`, `mysql` eat the loop's stdin",
            "Loop then exits after a single iteration",
            "Fix with `ssh -n`, or add `< /dev/null`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Spacing and Operators",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Wrong",
            "Right",
            "Why"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`if [$x -eq 1]`",
              "`[ $x -eq 1 ]`",
              "`[$x` is no command"
            ],
            [
              "`[ $x = a ]`",
              "`[ \"$x\" = a ]`",
              "empty = unary error"
            ],
            [
              "`[[ 10 > 9 ]]`",
              "`(( 10 > 9 ))`",
              "string vs number"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`=` string POSIX, `==` string Bash, `-eq` numeric",
            "Mixing them compares the wrong thing, silently",
            "`[ \"$a\" == \"$b\" ]` works in Bash, errors under `dash`"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Silent Failures: Subshells, Globs & Arithmetic",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Subshell Counter"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "count=0",
            "grep x f | while read l; do ((count++)); done",
            "echo $count   # 0 - the pipe made a subshell"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Loops and Quoting"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`for f in $(ls)` breaks on spaces; `for f in *` is correct",
            "Unquoted `$f` in the body re-splits it — always `\"$f\"`",
            "Forgetting `-r` on `read` mangles paths and escapes",
            "Body commands stealing stdin: loop exits after one pass"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Empty Glob"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No match: `*.txt` stays literal, loop runs once",
            "Guard the body with `[[ -e $f ]] || continue`",
            "Or set `shopt -s nullglob` and get zero passes"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Arithmetic Landmines"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "set -e",
            "i=0; (( i++ ))   # status 1, script aborts",
            "(( 08 + 1 ))     # value too great for base",
            "(( 10#08 + 1 ))  # 9, correct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "case Wreckage"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A `*)` before specific patterns swallows everything",
            "A missing `;;` merges branches into a syntax error",
            "The error points at the NEXT `)`, not the broken branch"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Status, Not Output",
          "text": "`if cmd > /dev/null` tests the exit status, not whether anything was printed. To test the output use `[[ -n $(cmd) ]]`."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "if cmd >/dev/null; then     # tests STATUS",
            "if [[ -n $(cmd) ]]; then    # tests OUTPUT"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why does my counter print 0?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "The `|` put the loop body in a subshell",
            "Its variable writes died with that subshell",
            "Feed the loop with `< <(cmd)` instead"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "while read l; do ((c++)); done < <(grep x f)",
            "echo $c   # correct now"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Let a Machine Find These",
          "items": [
            "`shellcheck script.sh` catches nearly this whole page",
            "Mechanical, fast, and it explains every code",
            "Wire it into CI, before review rather than after"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "If `b` fails, `c` runs too — a classic silent bug",
            "Braces expand before variables; use C-style `for`",
            "Always put a `sleep` inside a polling loop"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
