        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Dispatch one value over many patterns: `case`, `*)` last",
            "Need loop variables to survive: keep pipes out of loops",
            "Use `< <(cmd)`, or `mapfile -t arr < file` for whole files"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Defensive Header"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "#!/usr/bin/env bash",
            "set -euo pipefail",
            "IFS=$'\\n\\t'"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Truthiness Recap",
          "text": "Exit `0` is true everywhere — except inside `(( ))`, where numeric `0` is false. That one inversion causes most of the confusion."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Quoting Rule of Thumb"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Quote everything by default",
            "Except a glob or regex on the right of `[[ ]]`",
            "And except the word in `case $var in`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`128+N` encodes death by signal `N`",
            "Capture it at once: `rc=$?` — next command overwrites it"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "set -e, Exit Codes & Verification",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`-e` abort on non-zero, `-u` on unset variable",
            "`pipefail` reports the LAST (rightmost) failing pipe stage",
            "Tests inside `if`/`while`/`&&`/`!` never trip `-e`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Status Codes to Recognise"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`127`",
              "command not found"
            ],
            [
              "`126`",
              "found, not executable"
            ],
            [
              "`130`",
              "Ctrl-C, 128+2"
            ],
            [
              "`137`",
              "SIGKILL, 128+9"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Retry With Backoff",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "n=0",
            "until cmd; do",
            "  (( ++n >= 5 )) && exit 1",
            "  sleep $(( 2 ** n ))",
            "done"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Verify Before You Ship"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Tool",
            "Checks"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`bash -n`",
              "syntax only, no run"
            ],
            [
              "`bash -x`",
              "trace every expansion"
            ],
            [
              "`shellcheck`",
              "lint, most gotchas here"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "One-Line Recall",
          "items": [
            "`if cmd` for status, `[[ ]]` for text, `(( ))` for numbers",
            "`for` for lists, `while read` for lines, `case` for patterns",
            "Quote it unless it is meant to be a pattern"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. What They Compare"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Inside `[[ ]]`, `<` and `>` compare ==strings==, never numbers",
            "They answer: which word sorts first?",
            "So `[[ 10 > 9 ]]` is **false** — `\"1\"` precedes `\"9\"`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[[ abc < abd ]]   # true",
            "[[ B < a ]]       # ASCII order",
            "[[ 10 < 9 ]]      # true, strings!"
          ],
          "note": "text order, not numeric order"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. No Escaping in [[ ]]",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[[ ]]` is a ==keyword==, so Bash parses it before redirection",
            "`<` and `>` stay operators — write them bare",
            "No quoting, no backslash, no surprise file created"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "if [[ $name > $max ]]; then",
            "  max=$name",
            "fi   # plain < and > work"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "[ a \\< b ]     # compares",
            "[ a '<' b ]    # also compares",
            "[ a < b ]      # opens file b!"
          ],
          "note": "unescaped < is a redirection"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Lexicographic < and >",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. But [ ] Needs \\<",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[` is a ==command==, so `<` is parsed as a **redirect**",
            "`[ a < b ]` redirects stdin from the file `b`",
            "It **errors** if `b` is missing, and tests only `a`",
            "The mirror `[ a > b ]` is what creates/truncates `b`",
            "Escape it: `[ a \\< b ]`, or quote it: `[ a '<' b ]`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Side by Side"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Means"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`[[ a < b ]]`",
              "string compare"
            ],
            [
              "`[ a \\< b ]`",
              "string compare"
            ],
            [
              "`[ a < b ]`",
              "**redirect**, not a test"
            ],
            [
              "`(( a < b ))`",
              "numeric compare"
            ]
          ],
          "highlightRow": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Escaping Is the Tell",
          "text": "If you are typing `\\<`, you are in `[ ]`. In `[[ ]]` the backslash is wrong — it makes Bash compare against the literal `\\<`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Locale Decides Order"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`[[ ]]` sorts by the current ==LC_COLLATE==, not raw bytes",
            "Under `en_US` the order folds case: a, A, b, B",
            "`LC_ALL=C` restores plain ASCII: all caps before lowercase"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "LC_COLLATE=C",
            "[[ B < a ]]    # true, bytes",
            "LC_COLLATE=en_US.UTF-8",
            "[[ B < a ]]    # false, aAbB"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Gaps Worth Knowing"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "There is __no__ `<=` or `>=` — negate with `!` instead",
            "`[ ]` has no `<` at all in strict POSIX `sh`",
            "For numbers always reach for `(( ))` or `-lt` / `-gt`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "qa",
          "q": "Why did my test create an empty file?",
          "a_bullets": [
            "You wrote `>` unescaped inside single `[ ]`",
            "Bash redirected stdout and truncated that name",
            "Use `[[ ]]`, or escape it as `\\>`"
          ],
          "example_code": [
            "[ \"$a\" \\> \"$b\" ]   # safe in [ ]",
            "[[ $a > $b ]]      # safe in [[ ]]"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Quick Recall",
          "items": [
            "`[[ ]]`: bare `<` and `>`, sorts like `sort`",
            "`[ ]`: escape as `\\<` or the shell redirects",
            "`(( ))`: the only place `<` means arithmetic",
            "Set `LC_ALL=C` when you need stable byte order"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "subtitle": "Sorting Text, Not Math"
    }
  ],
  "caption": {
    "text": "Part 2 of BASH CONTROL FLOW. `[[ 10 > 9 ]]` is false, and that one line has broken more deploy scripts than any missing semicolon. This handbook walks the whole branching surface of Bash — exit status as the real engine, `[` vs `[[` vs `(( ))`, for/while/until/case, and the single correct way to read a file line by line. Ten pages of dense field notes, plus the gotchas that corrupt scripts without ever printing an error.",
